    13	
    14	## What the family asks
    15	
    16	An instance gives distinct cycle lengths `j`, each with multiplicity one, and
    17	sets `N = 1 + sum(j-1)`.  The solver must count equivalence classes of minimal
    18	factorizations of `(1 2 ... N)` having precisely those factor lengths.  Two
    19	factorizations are equivalent when adjacent disjoint (hence commuting) factors
    20	are swapped.  The solver may return the integer count, or a short increasing
    21	list of integers whose product is that count.  `verify` recomputes the count
    22	from the type vector with exact integer arithmetic and checks equality; it
    23	never reads the planted answer.
    24	
    25	The generator is theorem-backed, not solve-and-plant.  Theorem 1 identifies
    26	the classes with rooted plane trees whose internal degrees are `2j`.
    27	After removing the distinguished root leaf, the internal outdegrees are
    28	`2j-1`, and the cycle lemma counts the corresponding Łukasiewicz words.  If
    29	`R=sum(j-1)` and there are `m` distinct types, this gives
    30	
    31	```text
    32	H = (2R+m)! / (2R+1)! = (2R+2)(2R+3)...(2R+m).
    33	```
    34	
    35	The submitted witness is those `m-1` consecutive factors.  This is a
    36	composition-of-identities certificate and avoids asking a model to multiply a
    37	large integer by hand.
