-# Exact counts of inequivalent minimal cycle factorizations
-
-| Profile | Value |
-|---|---|
-| Paper | Berkolaiko, Harrison, Novaes, [*On inequivalent factorizations of a cycle*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.3476) |
-| Track | **B** — no-tool compression; this is not a Track A hardness claim |
-| Native domain / regime | combinatorics / finite discrete |
-| Computational core | other (exact enumeration) |
-| Certificate | exact symbolic product, with an exact-integer alternative |
-| Native objects | cycle-type vector, an n-cycle in a symmetric group, equivalence classes of minimal cycle factorizations |
-| Intended intuition | change of variables: factorization class → rooted plane tree → cyclic outdegree word |
-| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
-
-## What the family asks
-
-An instance gives distinct cycle lengths `j`, each with multiplicity one, and
-sets `N = 1 + sum(j-1)`.  The solver must count equivalence classes of minimal
-factorizations of `(1 2 ... N)` having precisely those factor lengths.  Two
-factorizations are equivalent when adjacent disjoint (hence commuting) factors
-are swapped.  The solver may return the integer count, or a short increasing
-list of integers whose product is that count.  `verify` recomputes the count
-from the type vector with exact integer arithmetic and checks equality; it
-never reads the planted answer.
-
-The generator is theorem-backed, not solve-and-plant.  Theorem 1 identifies
-the classes with rooted plane trees whose internal degrees are `2j`.
-After removing the distinguished root leaf, the internal outdegrees are
-`2j-1`, and the cycle lemma counts the corresponding Łukasiewicz words.  If
-`R=sum(j-1)` and there are `m` distinct types, this gives
-
-```text
-H = (2R+m)! / (2R+1)! = (2R+2)(2R+3)...(2R+m).
-```
-
-The submitted witness is those `m-1` consecutive factors.  This is a
-composition-of-identities certificate and avoids asking a model to multiply a
-large integer by hand.
-
-## Why Track B
-
-Section 1 fixes type, minimality, multiplication order, and commuting-factor
-equivalence.  Theorem 1 supplies the tree bijection and an explicit deletion
-reconstruction; this alone rules out an honest Track A claim for the tempting
-“order these cycles” family.  Lemma 1 fixes cycle orientation.  Theorem 2 and
-its proof in Section 4 give a multivariate generating-function recurrence.
-
-The general mechanical reference used here is generic prefix dynamic
-programming on Łukasiewicz outdegree words.  For `m` distinct types and rank
-`R`, it takes `O(2^m m R)` exact additions and `O(2^m R)` memory.  The recorded
-self-test ran it on eight shipping instances: it solved 8/8, using 36,364,288
-additions and 20.57 seconds total; repeated runs on this host ranged from 7.51
-to 37.38 seconds.  This is expected on Track B and is reported separately from
-attacks.  An efficient algorithm does exist and is not hidden: it is the
-`O(m)` cycle-lemma rising product above.  Its submitted symbolic form takes 33
-small operations at shipping size and avoids evaluating the large integer.
-Once that identity is recognized the problem is easy; the benchmark tests
-discovery of the change of variables, not worst-case complexity.
-
-## Worked demo
-
-The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders the following hand-scale instance:
-
-```text
-Exact count of inequivalent minimal cycle factorizations
-
-Work in the symmetric group on the labels 1,...,11.
-Let C be the cycle (1 2 ... 11).  Permutations are composed
-right-to-left: in sigma_m ... sigma_1, sigma_1 acts first.
-
-A cycle factorization of C is a product sigma_m ... sigma_1 = C.
-Its type records how many factors have each cycle length.  It is
-minimal when sum over its factors of (cycle_length - 1) equals
-11 - 1.  Two minimal factorizations are equivalent when one
-can be obtained from the other by repeatedly swapping adjacent
-disjoint cycles; disjoint cycles commute.
-
-Here m = 2.  There must be exactly one factor of each of these
-distinct lengths (the displayed order is irrelevant):
-  7 5
-Their sum of (length-1) values is 10, so minimality is exact.
-
-Find the exact number H of equivalence classes of such minimal
-factorizations.  Give one of the following exact certificates as JSON:
-1. {"value": V}, where V is the integer H and 0 <= V < 61;
-2. {"product": [a1]}, containing exactly one integer a1,
-   with 2 <= a1 <= 61,
-   and its exact product is H.  Order and distinctness are part of
-   this canonical product format; repeats are not allowed.
-
-Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON object
-in exactly one of the two formats above.
-Example format: <answer>{"product":[2]}</answer>
-Output nothing else inside the tags.
-```
-
-The answer is `<answer>{"product":[22]}</answer>`.  It is genuinely
-solvable on paper after the tree/cycle-word observation:
-`verify(inst, {"product": [22]}) == (True, "ok")`.
-A corrupted answer gives
-`verify(inst, {"product": []}) == (False, "product factor list is empty")`.
-
-## Difficulty presets
-
-| Preset | `n` (largest available cycle length) | Distinct types `m` | Product atoms | Status |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| demo | 7 | 2 | 1 | hand-solvable illustration |
-| easy | 24 | 7 | 6 | awaits oracle scoring |
-| medium | 40 | 9 | 8 | awaits oracle scoring |
-| hard | 64 | 11 | 10 | configured shipping preset |
-
-Increasing `n` enlarges the coefficient/rank range, the declared candidate
-space, and the generic DP cost while leaving the witness length fixed.
-`escalate` multiplies this ambient range by four before considering any longer
-answer.
-
-## Gate results
-
-| Gate | Result | Measurement |
-|---|---|---|
-| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset–seed planted certificates verified; JSON round-trip checked |
-| G2 | pass | six corruptions rejected with six distinct reasons |
-| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose and Markdown; garbage returned `None` |
-| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware random candidates valid; space size `51,453,109,464,148,470,609,508,424,937,634` |
-| G5 | pass | shipping density sample 0/200,000; reference DP 36,364,288 additions, 20.57 s, 8/8 solved; demo has exactly 2 valid certificate objects |
-| G6 | pass | all five attacks 0/8; reference algorithm separately solved 8/8 as Track B requires |
-| G7 | pass | doubled `n`, larger candidate space, same 10-atom answer |
-| G8 | pass | 60 reorder/composition invariance checks, 60 carried-certificate checks, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
-| G9(c) | pass | 53 characters, about 14 tokens, 10 atoms, 33 intended-route operations |
-
-The failing attacks were largest-input-length product, smallest admissible
-factors, independent-open-slot power, an off-by-one rising product, and 256
-structure-aware random restarts per seed.  Factors are all sampled by the same
-rule; there is no planted-versus-decoy distributional marker.
-
-## Oracle loop and G9 arms
-
-The mandatory harness was invoked, but the configured OpenRouter key returned
-HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every retry.  Per the harness rules these are
-errors, not model failures, so no hardness verdict can honestly be claimed.
-The harness-owned files retain all error records.
-
-| Run | Preset | Scored solved/attempts | Recorded calls | Outcome |
-|---|---|---:|---:|---|
-| bare | easy | 0/0 | 4 errors | blocked by OpenRouter quota |
-| structural hint | hard | 0/0 | 4 errors | blocked by OpenRouter quota |
-| placebo hint | hard | 0/0 | 4 errors | blocked by OpenRouter quota |
-
-Thus `hinted - placebo` is not estimable yet; the zero currently recorded in
-the report is a neutral placeholder over zero scored attempts, not evidence
-that the hint helps or fails to help.  Re-run all three arms after replenishing
-the OpenRouter key.  The structural hint only names the cyclic-word invariant;
-it does not state the product formula or an algorithm.
-
-## Use
-
-```python
-from gen_0809_3476 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
-
-inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
-print(render(inst))
-ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
-assert ok, reason
-```
-
-From the repository root, after a successful oracle rerun:
-
-```bash
-bash scripts/emit.sh 0809.3476 20 hard
-```
-
-## Caveats
-
-- This module is not ready to submit until the bare and G9 harness calls obtain
-  valid provider responses.  Error-only transcripts establish no LLM hardness.
-- The closed form is an efficient `O(m)` big-integer algorithm and is also the
-  intended compact route.  The measured DP is the honest generic mechanical
-  alternative from the recurrence, not the asymptotically strongest known
-  algorithm.  This distinction is why the family is Track B.
-- Zero hits estimates density only under the declared uniform prior over the
-  two bounded, fully shape-constrained certificate forms.  It is not a proof
-  of a Bayesian model-success probability, and alternate product certificates
-  may exist.
-- The panel did not test a CAS with generating-function recognition, a language
-  model with tools, or every possible arithmetic ansatz.  Any of those can make
-  the family easy, as can recognizing the rooted-plane-tree cycle lemma.
-- `canonical_key` quotients input reordering.  Cycle lengths are semantic type
-  data, so arbitrary permutations of the length values are not isomorphisms.
-- `gvlib` is importable when present, but this family intentionally needs only
-  standard-library exact integers; there are no floats in verification.
