    12	| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    13	
    14	## What the family asks
    15	
    16	An instance gives distinct cycle lengths `j`, each with multiplicity one, and
    17	sets `N = 1 + sum(j-1)`. The solver must count equivalence classes of minimal
    18	factorizations of `(1 2 ... N)` having precisely those factor lengths. Two
    19	factorizations are equivalent when adjacent disjoint, hence commuting, factors
    20	are swapped. The answer may be the integer count or a short increasing list of
    21	integers whose product is that count. `verify` recomputes the count from the
    22	type vector with exact integer arithmetic and never reads the planted answer.
    23	
    24	The generator is theorem-backed, not solve-and-plant. Theorem 1 identifies the
    25	classes with rooted plane trees whose internal degrees are `2j`. Removing the
    26	distinguished root leaf gives internal outdegrees `2j-1`; the cycle lemma counts
    27	the resulting Łukasiewicz words. If `R=sum(j-1)` and there are `m` distinct
    28	types, then
    29	
    30	```text
    31	H = (2R+m)! / (2R+1)! = (2R+2)(2R+3)...(2R+m).
    32	```
    33	
    34	The planted witness is those `m-1` consecutive factors. This composition of
    35	identities keeps the answer writable without asking the model to multiply a
    36	large integer.
    37	
    38	## Why Track B
    39	
    40	Section 1 fixes type, minimality, multiplication order, and commuting-factor
    41	equivalence. Theorem 1 supplies the tree bijection and an explicit deletion
    42	reconstruction; this makes the tempting “order these planted cycles” family
    43	constructively easy and rules out Track A. Lemma 1 fixes cycle orientation.
    44	Theorem 2 and Section 4 supply the recurrence used by the mechanical reference.
    45	
    46	Generic prefix dynamic programming on Łukasiewicz outdegree words takes
    47	`O(2^m m R)` exact additions and `O(2^m R)` memory. On eight shipping instances
    48	it solved 8/8, using 361,472 additions total (45,184 average) and 0.58 seconds in
    49	the recorded self-test; repeated runs ranged from about 0.1 to 0.7 seconds.
    50	This success is expected and is separate from the attack panel. The efficient
    51	compact algorithm is also stated openly: recognize the tree/cycle-word identity
    52	and form the rising product in `O(m)` time. Its symbolic answer needs 21 small
    53	exact operations at shipping size. This tests discovery of the compression,
    54	not worst-case complexity.
    55	
    56	## Worked demo
    57	
    58	The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders this hand-scale instance:
    59	
    60	```text
    61	Exact count of inequivalent minimal cycle factorizations
    62	
    63	Work in the symmetric group on the labels 1,...,11.
    64	Let C be the cycle (1 2 ... 11).  Permutations are composed
    65	right-to-left: in sigma_m ... sigma_1, sigma_1 acts first.
    66	
    67	A cycle factorization of C is a product sigma_m ... sigma_1 = C.
    68	Its type records how many factors have each cycle length.  It is
    69	minimal when sum over its factors of (cycle_length - 1) equals
    70	11 - 1.  Two minimal factorizations are equivalent when one
    71	can be obtained from the other by repeatedly swapping adjacent
    72	disjoint cycles; disjoint cycles commute.
    73	
    74	Here m = 2.  There must be exactly one factor of each of these
    75	distinct lengths (the displayed order is irrelevant):
    76	  7 5
    77	Their sum of (length-1) values is 10, so minimality is exact.
    78	
    79	Find the exact number H of equivalence classes of such minimal
    80	factorizations.  Give one of the following exact certificates as JSON:
    81	1. {"value": V}, where V is the integer H and 0 <= V < 61;
    82	2. {"product": [a1]}, containing exactly one integer a1,
    83	   with 2 <= a1 <= 61,
    84	   and its exact product is H.  Order and distinctness are part of
    85	   this canonical product format; repeats are not allowed.
    86	
    87	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON object
    88	in exactly one of the two formats above.
    89	Example format: <answer>{"product":[2]}</answer>
    90	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    91	```
    92	
    93	The answer is `<answer>{"product":[22]}</answer>`. It is hand-solvable
    94	after the tree/cycle-word observation:
    95	`verify(inst, {"product": [22]}) == (True, "ok")`. A corruption gives
    96	`verify(inst, {"product": []}) == (False, "product factor list is empty")`.
    97	
    98	## Difficulty presets
    99	
   100	| Preset | Maximum length `n` | Distinct types `m` | Product atoms | Status |
   101	|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
   102	| demo | 7 | 2 | 1 | hand-solvable; skipped by hardening |
   103	| easy | 24 | 7 | 6 | **ships; bare oracle held 0/3** |
   104	| medium | 40 | 9 | 8 | not reached after `easy` held |
   105	| hard | 64 | 11 | 10 | not reached after `easy` held |
   106	
   107	Increasing `n` enlarges the coefficient/rank range and generic DP cost.
   108	`escalate` multiplies this ambient range by four while holding answer length
   109	fixed.
   110	
   111	## Gate results
   112	
   113	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
   114	|---|---|---|
   115	| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset–seed certificates verified; JSON round-trip checked |
   116	| G2 | pass | six corruptions rejected with six distinct reasons |
   117	| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose and Markdown; garbage returned `None` |
   118	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware candidates valid; space `5,736,085,959,633,562` |
   119	| G5 | pass | density 0/200,000; DP 361,472 additions, 0.58 s, 8/8 solved; demo exact count two |
   120	| G6 | pass | all five in-context attacks 0/8; reference algorithm separately solved 8/8 |
   121	| G7 | pass | `n=24→48`, larger space, same six-atom answer |
   122	| G8 | pass | 60 invariance and 60 carried-certificate checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
   123	| G9(c) | pass | 37 characters, about 10 tokens, six atoms, 21 intended operations |
   124	
   125	The failing attacks were largest-input-length product, smallest admissible
   126	factors, independent-open-slot power, an off-by-one rising product, and 256
   127	structure-aware random restarts per seed. Every cycle length is sampled by the
   128	same rule; there is no planted-versus-decoy marker.
   129	
   130	## Oracle loop
   131	
   132	The harness returned `hardened` at `easy` without escalation. The third call
   133	was a valid HTTP response but emitted no answer after using the 32,000-token
   134	completion budget; this is recorded separately from the two wrong answers.
   135	
   136	| Preset | Model | Seed | Solved | Reason |
   137	|---|---|---:|---:|---|
   138	| easy | `google/gemini-3.8-flash` | 1037224055 | no | parsed product had the wrong exact value |
   139	| easy | `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` | 1882228916 | no | parsed product had the wrong exact value |
   140	| easy | `google/gemini-3.8-flash` | 776643309 | no | empty length-limited response |
   141	
   142	## G9 arms
   143	
   144	| Arm | Solved / scored attempts | Outcome |
   145	|---|---:|---|
   146	| bare | 0/3 | hardened |
   147	| structural hint | 0/3 | hardened |
   148	| placebo | 0/1 | incomplete; four later calls were HTTP 403 quota errors |
   149	
   150	The observed hinted-minus-placebo rate is `0.0`, so there is no observed
   151	structural lift. That conclusion is weak because the placebo arm has only one
   152	scored attempt: the OpenRouter key reached its total limit before the other two
   153	could run. Errors are retained in `g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl` and are not
   154	counted as failures. The hint names only the cyclic-word invariant. Answer size
   155	is 37 characters (about 10 tokens), six atoms; the intended route uses 21 exact
   156	operations.
   157	
   158	## Use
   159	
   160	```python
   161	from gen_0809_3476 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
   162	
   163	inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
   164	print(render(inst))
   165	ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
   166	assert ok, reason
   167	```
   168	
   169	From the repository root:
   170	
   171	```bash
   172	bash scripts/emit.sh 0809.3476 20 easy
   173	```
   174	
   175	## Caveats
   176	
   177	- Bare hardening is complete, but the non-gating placebo diagnostic is short by
   178	  two scored attempts because external OpenRouter quota was exhausted. HTTP
   179	  errors are not hardness evidence and were not counted.
   180	- The closed form is an efficient `O(m)` algorithm and the compact route. The
   181	  DP is the generic route from the recurrence, not the strongest known method;
   182	  this distinction is why the family is Track B.
   183	- Zero random hits estimates density only under the declared uniform prior over
   184	  the two bounded, shape-constrained certificate forms. It is not a Bayesian
   185	  model-success probability, and alternate product certificates may exist.
   186	- The panel did not test CAS generating-function recognition, a model with
   187	  tools, or every arithmetic ansatz. Recognizing the cycle lemma makes it easy.
   188	- `canonical_key` quotients input reordering. Cycle lengths are semantic data,
   189	  so arbitrary changes to their values are not isomorphisms.
   190	- `gvlib` is imported when present, but this family only needs standard-library
   191	  exact integers; verification uses no floating point.

